#This does not constitute as THE .env file, but rather a template for the .env file
PGUSER=pguser
PGPASSWORD=pgpassword
PGHOST=pghost
PGDATABASE=pgdatabase
PGPORT=pgport

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUNNING THIS DASHBOARD LOCALLY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dashboard reads from TWO places for a given scenario id, and needs both:
#   1. the analytics tables in Postgres  (Summary, Model, PkLnQ, rPkLnQ, PdStQ,
#      FG_Cm)
#   2. that scenario's solved workbook at
#      ../RIC-BGO-Tool/excelFiles/solved/<id>.xlsx
#
# The local RIC-BGO-Tool dev database cannot be used: its analytics tables are
# empty, because only the real Solver.py populates them and local development
# runs a stub. Locally-solved workbooks do not work either -- the stub copies
# the input template through, so the sheets are named Cm_Master / ZIP_Master and
# there is no Model or Summary sheet, which is what dashboard.py reads.
#
# So: read from production (this app only ever SELECTs), and fetch one real
# solved workbook.
#
# 1. Python. requirements.txt pins versions that have no wheels for 3.13+, so
#    build the venv against 3.12:
#       uv venv --python 3.12 venv && source venv/bin/activate
#       uv pip install -r requirements.txt
#
# 2. Tunnel to the production database. Its port 5432 is closed to the
#    internet, which is deliberate -- reach it over SSH instead. 5434 is the
#    port the RIC tooling already uses for this (pgAdmin included), so one
#    tunnel serves everything. Leave this running in its own terminal:
#       ssh -N -L 5434:127.0.0.1:5432 bgo-server
#
# 3. Point THIS file's real counterpart (.env, gitignored) at the tunnel:
#       PGHOST=127.0.0.1
#       PGPORT=5434
#    Credentials stay as they are. The server keeps its own .env, so this change
#    is local only and cannot affect the deployed container.
#
# 4. Fetch a real solved workbook for whichever scenario you want to open:
#       scp bgo-server:~/RIC-BGO-Tool/excelFiles/solved/2650.xlsx \
#           ../RIC-BGO-Tool/excelFiles/solved/
#
# 5. Run it. The scenario id comes from the URL, as it does in production where
#    the BGO Tool links here:
#       streamlit run dashboard.py
#       open http://localhost:8501/?id=2650
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
